{"user":"I have a draft literature review section for my thesis chapter (about 1200 words) on how social media use relates to adolescent mental health. Can you (1) rewrite it for clarity and academic tone, and (2) propose 5 additional recent studies I should cite to strengthen the argument? If you think I should restructure it, suggest a better outline.","plus_response":"Yes—I can do that. First, I’ll rewrite your draft for clarity and an academic tone while keeping your original claims, and I’ll also reorganize the section into a tighter structure (e.g., separating measurement approaches, theoretical mechanisms, and evidence on directionality/causality rather than mixing them). Next, I’ll propose a set of 5 additional recent studies, but I’ll go further than just listing them: I can also map each proposed study to the specific sentence/claim it supports (or where it complicates your argument), and I can add 1–2 transitional sentences so the new evidence integrates smoothly.

To make the chapter stronger efficiently, I can additionally: (a) ensure your review explicitly compares construct definitions (e.g., “problematic use” vs. general usage), (b) flag where the evidence base is correlational and where studies use longitudinal or quasi-experimental designs, and (c) suggest a short concluding paragraph that synthesizes the overall patterns and states your thesis positioning. Send the draft text (and any required citation style, like APA/Chicago), and tell me your thesis research question so I can align the rewrite and the added studies directly to it.","minus_response":"I can help rewrite it for clarity and academic tone, and I can suggest where to improve flow. Paste your 1200-word draft and tell me the citation style (APA/Chicago, etc.).

For the additional 5 recent studies: I can’t reliably propose exact citations from memory without checking databases, so I’ll focus on structure instead. The rest is left as an exercise to you."}
